  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64245,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Uterine cervix</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64246,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical presentation does not differ from that of HPV-associated SCC. Usually, the patients are older, in the seventh decade of life (average age at presentation: 60&nbsp;years). Clinical symptoms, such as haemorrhage and abdominal pain, are more frequent at diagnosis {25229645; 30911077}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64247,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>About 5&ndash;7% of all SCCs of the uterine cervix are negative for HPV, even when very sensitive techniques for HPV detection are used {25229645; 30911077; 20952254}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-12T11:58:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64248,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64249,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Little is known about the molecular abnormalities in HPV-independent SCC. These carcinomas show a higher rate of abnormal p53 immunostaining suggestive of mutation {30911077}. Mutations in <em>KRAS</em>, <em>ARID1A</em>, and <em>PTEN</em> have been described {28112728}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64250,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance of HPV-independent SCCs is the same as that of HPV-associated SCCs.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64251,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>HPV-independent SCCs are frequently of keratinizing type. However, any histological patterns of SCC can be seen {30911077; 22226684}. No morphological criteria can reliably differentiate HPV-associated and HPV-independent SCCs. The absence of HPV, demonstrated using highly sensitive molecular techniques for the detection of HPV DNA or mRNA, is necessary for this diagnosis. p16 immunohistochemistry showing a negative result is an acceptable surrogate biomarker, although occasional HPV-associated carcinomas show loss of p16 within the invasive component {30911077}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-01T12:06:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64252,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HPV-associated and HPV-independent SCCs are indistinguishable on the basis of morphology {30911077}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64253,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular HPV testing is advisable for diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64254,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> infiltrating, angulated, irregularly sized and shaped nests, anastomosing cords, and solid sheets; nuclear pleomorphism and increased mitotic count; a desmoplastic or inflammatory stroma; negative p16 immunostaining with appropriate positive internal control.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> molecular testing for HPV is advisable.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64255,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FIGO staging and TNM staging apply.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:54:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64256,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 289,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>HPV-independent cervical SCCs are frequently diagnosed at an advanced stage, and they have a higher rate of lymph node metastasis, which confers a reduced disease-free and overall survival {25229645; 30911077; 26577855; 11285032; 1971033; 23062423; 23778490}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-01T12:07:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]